## Getting Started  

I need to know what the ambient humidity and temperature is for my tea plants, which thrive in high humidity conditions. Unfortunately, most houses are uncomfortably dry for the plants, so I have been supplementing humidity with a humidifier. Right now, I have a cheap hygrometer and thermometer from Amazon, but I wanted to have a more elegant option that logs the data so that I can visualize it. This ties into my tea plant cultivation experiments, which are detailed here (link) and here (link).

Since I’ve been kindling an interest in electronics and coding, I decided to build a small hygrometer monitoring station myself. Here’s what I used:
(1) ESP8266 NodeMCU
(1) SH1106 OLED
(1) BME280 sensor
Jumper wires
Breadboard
MicroUSB(to upload code and later to power device)
The idea is to log temperature, humidity, and pressure (since the BME280 collects all three anyway) data to ThingSpeak for later analysis.  

v1 (MVP 1): Hygrometer that displays the reading on a display
V2 (MVP 2): Takes data and uploads it to the cloud

## First Steps
This project is mostly an experiment—I wanted to get comfortable working with an OLED display first, then move on to integrating a sensor and sending data to the cloud later.

### The OLED Display  

The first step was getting the SH1106 OLED display working with the ESP8266. Since I am a complete beginner with electronics, I relied heavily on the guides posted on Random Nerd Tutorials (link).

To be honest, I probably spent four hours struggling with the wiring on this sucker. I don’t know if it was just my lack of experience or if there is an issue with the new breadboard that I bought, because the old breadboard that I had on hand managed to fix the issues I was having.

Using the U8g2 library, I was able to display text easily. Here’s the basic setup:  

```cpp
#include <Wire.h>
#include <U8g2lib.h>

U8G2_SH1106_128X64_NONAME_F_HW_I2C u8g2(U8G2_R0, U8X8_PIN_NONE, D1, D2);

void setup() {
  u8g2.begin();
}

void loop() {
  u8g2.clearBuffer();
  u8g2.setFont(u8g2_font_ncenB08_tr);
  u8g2.drawStr(0, 10, "Hello, ESP8266!");
  u8g2.sendBuffer();
  delay(1000);
}
//end code
Seeing "Hello, ESP8266!" on the screen was a nice milestone!


## A Small Setback: The BME280 Sensor
Next, I wanted to connect a BME280 sensor to read temperature, humidity, and pressure. Unfortunately, I made a classic beginner mistake—I accidentally ordered an unsoldered BME280 module. Since I don’t know how to solder (yet), I had to put the sensor integration on hold while I wait for a pre-soldered module to arrive.
This did give me some time to read up on I2C communication and how multiple devices can share the same bus (D1/D2 on the ESP8266). Once my new sensor arrives, I’ll update my code to integrate the BME280 and send data to ThingSpeak.
